   125	
   126	## Gate results
   127	
   128	### Gate A — PASS
   129	
   130	- A1: The entry claim loads the exact generated body and resolves the actual
   131	  module binding. The only operational bridge has a bridge-free universal
   132	  connection theorem. Replacing its body with `.Stmts` makes that theorem fail.
   133	- A2: The bridge's connection theorem covers `item2`, `closest`, continuation,
   134	  bindings, and every framed configuration cell. No exception, heap, stack,
   135	  output, return, or control effect is discarded.
   136	- A3: The complete environment, builtins namespace, closure binding/body,
   137	  target, loop body, continuation, and local scope are pinned. The bridge and
   138	  connection theorem accept the same context.
   139	- A4: Total functions have exhaustive constructor or complementary Boolean
   140	  cases. Recursive equations descend. The only off-domain recognizer lemma was
   141	  removed and replaced with the guarded true direction.
   142	- A5: Length-two float lists realize the entry precondition. The false
   143	  three-element result mutation fails with a stuck result residual.
   144	
   145	### Gate B — FAIL
   146	
   147	- B1: The formal domain aligns with `List[float]` of length at least two.
   148	- B2/B3: Symbolic `subF`, `absF`, and `floatLt` are intentionally opaque in the
   149	  supplied Haskell semantics. K proves the exact exhaustive scan in terms of
   150	  those primitives, but `spec.k` does not separately formalize and prove an
   151	  order-theoretic global-minimum predicate or Python/K equivalence for special
   152	  IEEE values such as NaN. The human-facing "closest" conclusion is therefore
   153	  conditional on the named float contracts and empirically supported, not a
   154	  fully formal consequence inside K.
   155	- B4: For ordinary finite floats, the implementation, examples, and independent
   156	  oracle agree. No implementation/specification discrepancy was observed.
   157	
   158	This Gate B limitation determines the `SOUND-BUT-LIMITED` headline.
   159	
   160	### Gate C — PASS
   161	
   162	Every unproved primitive and all evidence are listed below with artifacts,
   163	commands, scope, oracle, and actual outcome. Formal, conditional, empirical,
   164	and excluded conclusions are separated in this report.
   165	
   166	## Trust boundary
   167	
   168	| Component | Effect and dependents | Status/evidence |
   169	|---|---|---|
   170	| Reference `subF(Float, Float)` | Produces candidate/current distances; affects `candidateWins`, both folds, and final result | Fixed external primitive, opaque to Haskell and concrete under LLVM. Covered on finite witnesses by `smoke.py`/`krun` and the CPython differential suite. |
   171	| Reference `absF(Float)` | Produces absolute distances; affects the same claims | Same conditional trust boundary and evidence. |
   172	| Reference `floatLt(Float, Float)` | Selects smaller-first order and winning candidates; affects control and result | Same conditional trust boundary and evidence. No total-order law for NaN is assumed in K. |
   173	| Supplied `MPY` rules, K backend, and SMT engine | Defines and checks all execution/proof steps | Foundational toolchain/semantics trust. The reference files were not modified. |
   174	
   175	The inner-loop operational bridge is not a trust assumption: its complete
   176	domain is connected to fixed execution by a separate `#Top` theorem.
   177	
   178	## Empirical evidence
   179	
   180	`smoke.py` contains the two prompt examples plus negative/unsorted and
   181	descending two-element cases. The command:
   182	
   183	```bash
   184	python3 py2mpy.py smoke.py > smoke.mpy
   185	krun smoke.mpy --definition runtime-kompiled
   186	```
   187	
   188	uses the supplied LLVM semantics as the executable oracle; all assertions were
   189	consumed, final `<k>` was `.K`, and `<exit-code>` was `0`.
   190	
   191	`differential_test.py` uses `itertools.combinations`, independently computes
   192	the minimum absolute pair distance, checks multiplicity through distinct
   193	positions, and checks smaller-first ordering. It covers six fixed boundary and
   194	prompt cases plus 1000 seeded random finite lists of lengths 2 through 8:
   195	
   196	```text
